# EXPECT_RUNTIME_EXIT: 0
# EXPECT_STDOUT_EXACT: "Mostly Harmless\n"
# EXPECT_NO_LEAKS
# EXPECT_DUMP_SLIR: test_slir_assert_holds.slir
#
# An assert switches on its condition: a zero goes to the failing block, and any other
# value goes on to the next block (ir.md 8.9). The failing block evaluates the message and
# ends in the trap RE2026, with two operands: the position of the assert, and the message.
# A bare assert gives the empty string as its message, and its position has no `: `. The
# message `"n is {n}"` is an interpolation that only the failing block builds, and the
# trap drops nothing. A condition local ends at the top of the two blocks.

fn check(bool ok, i32 n) ~:
    assert(ok)
    assert(n > 0, "n is {n}")
    assert(n < 10, "n is small")

fn main() i32:
    check(true, 3)
    println("Mostly Harmless")
    return 0
